description:
Why this is good: metadata.rs:410:

  let patch = match serde_json::from_value::<PatchObject>(patch_val) {
      Ok(p) => p,
      Err(e) => {
          not_updated.insert(id_str, set_error_value(&SetError::new(SetErrorType::InvalidPatch).with_description(e.to_string())));
          continue;
      }
  };

This leverages PatchObject's serde-defined invariants. A wrong-shape patch (e.g. a string, an array) produces a serde error that becomes invalidPatch with description. If PatchObject's shape evolves (e.g. adds a new constraint), this code adapts automatically.

The alternative (which would be 'simpler') is:
  if !patch_val.is_object() { return invalidPatch; }
  // then trust patch_val is an Object Map

…which is verbose and brittle. If PatchObject's constraint surface grows (e.g. value-side validation), the manual check doesn't catch it.

The current pattern uses the type system to enforce the contract. Test (set_update_non_object_patch_returns_invalid_patch, metadata.rs:856) verifies the wire shape.

A future contributor might suggest 'just check is_object'. Defend: PatchObject IS the contract; let it speak for itself.
